  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to employ a hybrid genetic algorithm (GA) with adaptive mutation and crossover operators, combined with local refinement via separable convex optimization. This approach addresses the limitations of gradient-based methods by escaping local optima through global search while leveraging the efficiency of convex subproblems for final refinement. The key improvement lies in using a structured mutation mechanism that preserves constraint satisfaction and reduces overlap, balancing exploration and exploitation. The primary limitation is computational overhead from genetic operators, which is mitigated by early termination and population sizing.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points: step function with values in [0,1]\n- dx = 2.0 / n_points: grid spacing\n- target_sum = n_points / 2: required integral constraint\n- C\u2085 = max_k [\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx] * dx (exact objective)\n\nSurrogate Objective (for GA):\n- Use maximum overlap value directly as fitness metric\n- Penalize constraint violations with a quadratic term: \u03bb*(\u2211h - target_sum)^2\n\nConvex Subproblem (local refinement):\n- Relax the max operator to its largest value\n- Formulate as minimizing the maximum overlap via a Lagrangian dual\n\nConstraints:\n1. \u2211h[i] = target_sum\n2. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nParent validation:\n- If initial_h_values provided, normalize it to [0,1]^n_points\n- Check \u2211h \u2248 target_sum within 1e-6\n\nInitialization:\n- For n_points=300, generate 20 initial solutions:\n   a) If initial_h_values available, use it as a base population\n   b) Otherwise, generate random h vectors with uniform distribution\n   c) Normalize each by scaling to target_sum\n\nGA main loop (max 500 iterations):\nFor each generation:\na) Evaluate fitness: C\u2085 for each particle\nb) Apply tournament selection (size 5) to choose parents\nc) Perform mutation:\n   - For each h, apply:\n      i) Random segment inversion: select 5% of indices and flip values\n      ii) Global shift: add/subtract small value (\u00b10.05) to random segments\n      iii) Perturb 10% of segments with Gaussian noise (\u03c3=0.1)\nd) Crossover (single-point):\n   - Combine two parents to form a child\ne) Repair constraints:\n   - Normalize h to target_sum\n   - Clip values to [0,1]\n   - If \u2211h\u2260target_sum, rescale proportionally\nf) Replace worst 10% of population with new mutants\n\nLocal refinement phase:\n- Take top 5 GA solutions\n- For each, perform 10 iterations of:\n   a) Use CVXPY to minimize max_overlap with:\n      i) Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points\n      ii) Objective: minimize max( np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\") * dx )\n      iii) Constraint: \u2211h = target_sum\n   b) Use ECOS solver with \u03b5=1e-6\n   c) After refinement, re-evaluate with exact objective\n\nTermination:\n- Track best solution throughout\n- After GA completes, use final top solution\n- If time permits, perform final CVXPY refinement\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault Configuration:\n- n_points=300 (balances resolution and time)\n- GA population=20\n- Mutation rate=0.75 (probability per individual)\n- Crossover rate=0.8\n- Tournament size=5\n- Refinement iterations=10\n\nVariations:\n- Adaptive mutation: Increase mutation rate when C\u2085 stagnates\n- Multi-objective: Add diversity penalty to maintain population spread\n- Hybrid refinement: Use L-BFGS-B after CVXPY for faster convergence\n- Dynamic population: Adjust population size based on progress\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime Allocation:\n- 200s: GA phase (500 iterations with 20 population)\n- 150s: Refinement phase (5 solutions \u00d7 10 iterations)\n- 150s: Fallback and final checks\n\nRobustness Measures:\n- Maintain valid solutions via repair steps\n- Track best solution after every generation\n- Use exponential decay for mutation rates\n- Limit individual mutation complexity to O(n_points)\n- Store parent solution as fallback\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility Checks:\n- \u2211h \u2248 target_sum within 1e-6\n- All h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n- Correlation calculation uses mode=\"full\" with proper scaling\n- Ensure correlation length matches 2*n_points-1\n\nFinal Validation:\n- Re-compute C\u2085 using exact objective function\n- Ensure results match evaluator's calculation\n- Perform sanity checks:\n   a) Correlation length matches expected size\n   b) Overlap values are positive\n   c) Maximum overlap is correctly identified\n   d) Constraint satisfaction is within tolerance\n\nReturn Format:\n- h_values as list of floats in [0,1]\n- c5_bound as exact value\n- n_points as integer",
